  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Headline results</p>

      <div class="result-grid">
        <div class="metric">
          <strong>350 / 350</strong>
          <span>tokiMind scored recall</span>
        </div>

        <div class="metric">
          <strong>341 / 350</strong>
          <span>Full-context recall</span>
        </div>

        <div class="metric">
          <strong>66.60%</strong>
          <span>Lower provider-reported total tokens vs. full context</span>
        </div>

        <div class="metric">
          <strong>1,932 / 1,932</strong>
          <span>Experiment tasks succeeded</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Abstract</h2>

      <p>
        Long-running AI agents face a persistent-state tradeoff: bounded context
        can lose information outside the active window, while accumulated
        history increases prompt and reasoning burden and does not guarantee that
        currently valid state will dominate stale or contradictory history.
      </p>

      <p>
        This study evaluates tokiMind, a governed persistent-memory system that
        treats memory as mutable operational state with explicit provenance,
        authority, contradiction handling, supersession, freshness, revocation,
        and tenant isolation. A frozen 300-hour benchmark executed 1,932
        scheduled runs across accumulated full context, bounded context without
        persistent memory, and bounded context with tokiMind.
      </p>

      <p>
        Across 350 paired scored recall cases per cohort, tokiMind achieved
        350/350 accuracy, full context achieved 341/350, and the bounded-context
        negative control achieved 0/350. All nine discordant paired cases favored
        tokiMind.
      </p>

      <h2>Evidence and reproducibility</h2>

      <p>
        The public evidence package is deposited separately from the manuscript.
        It contains scrubbed frozen core evidence, operational evidence,
        run-level scored recall cases, checkpoint and entity-level summaries,
        security/governance evaluations, token and latency measurements,
        reliability summaries, checksums, and reproduction materials.
      </p>

      <p>
        The frozen experiment is identified by manifest
        <code>tokimind-longitudinal-v4-enterprise-300h-v1</code> and Git commit
        <code>6975e8344666e98ff71e355ebd4ff9e7b0fe08da</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>Scope</h2>

      <p>
        The work does not claim that persistent memory or memory governance was
        invented here, nor that tokiMind is universally superior across models,
        tasks, or deployment environments. The contribution is the evaluated
        governed-state system and its frozen longitudinal paired systems
        evaluation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
